Brief

    zelda64 manifest files (typically using the extension .zmf) are binary
    files that describe how a Nintendo 64 Zelda ROM can be created from a
    decompressed ROM. It contains all information in a single file that can be
    shared in the same manner a patch file can be shared.

    The file format encodes all multibyte values as big endian and all chunks
    must be aligned and padded to 16 bytes. The design of the file format is
    inspired by the IFF (Interchange File Format, see reference below) in how
    it stores its data.

Header

    All manifest files start with a 64 byte header.

        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+
        |  offset  |    type    |             Description              |
        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+
        |    0x00  |   char[4]  |  Magic constant ("Z64M")             |
        |    0x04  |  uint32_t  |  Format version                      |
        |    0x08  |   char[8]  |  Reserved                            |
        |    0x10  |   char[4]  |  Game code                           |
        |    0x14  |   uint8_t  |  Game version                        |
        |    0x15  |   char[3]  |  Reserved                            |
        |    0x18  |  uint64_t  |  Check code                          |
        |    0x20  |  char[32]  |  Program                             |
        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+

    The first field is always "Z64M", which must be validated by any proper
    implementation to ensure that the file is really a Z64M file.

    The second field is the version of the file format, packed into a double
    word like this: 0xMMMMmmmm. The current version is 0x00010000. Proper
    implementations should always be able to read files of the same major
    version or less, and reject files of a greater major version.

    The game code field is a 4 letter code of the created ROM, for example
    this field would be "CZLE" for the US release of Legend of Zelda: Ocarina
    of Time.

    The game version field is the biased version number of the game, exactly
    the same as it appeared in the ROM. For the 1.0 version of Ocarina of Time
    this value would be 0.

    The check code is the 64-bit check code of the ROM that is produced by the
    manifest file, which can be used for verification purposes. The check code
    is optional and may be zeroed out.

    The program field can optionally denote the program that created the
    manifest file. It must be a 32-byte NUL-terminated string. For example,
    manifests created by `decompress` from `libzelda64-utils` will set this to
    "libzelda64 0.1".

Chunks

    Following the header is zero or more chunks. Every chunk starts with a
    16-byte header.

        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+
        |  offset  |    type    |             Description              |
        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+
        |    0x00  |   char[4]  |  Magic                               |
        |    0x04  |  uint32_t  |  Length in bytes                     |
        |    0x08  |  uint32_t  |  CRC-32 checksum                     |
        |    0x0C  |   char[4]  |  Reserved                            |
        +----------+------------+--------------------------------------+

    Each chunk header begins a 4-byte magic to describe what kind of chunk it
    is. The first two bytes should be treated as a namespace for any extensions
    specific to an application. The "ZM" namespace is reserved for libzelda64.

    The only other mandatory field in the header is the length in bytes. This
    field contains the size of the chunk's data minus the 16 bytes for the
    header.

    The third field is an optional CRC-32 (CRC32/IEEE, see reference below)
    checksum of the data in the chunk.

    The last four bytes are reserved for future use.

    Every chunk begins aligned to 16 bytes in the file. Implementations can
    skip to the next chunk by aligning the length of the chunk:

        next = current + 16 + ((length + 15) & -16)

Check Code Chunk [ZMCC]

    The ZMCC chunk describes a list of valid ROMs that may be used as input
    for creating the ROM described in the manifest. Every ROM is described as
    a pair of check codes. The first check code of the pair is the compressed
    ROM's check code and the second check code is that of the decompressed ROM.

    Implementations should reject ROMs that have a mismatching check code. If
    the check code matches a compressed ROM, it must be decompressed first.

File Operations Chunk [ZMOP]

    The ZMOP chunk describes the operations that must be carried out by the
    compressor to produce the compressed ROM. It is encoded as an array of
    bytes whose indices correspond to their indices in the DMADATA.

    Each byte can be one of the following values:

        00  Skip the index and record an empty file
        01  Copy from the uncompressed ROM to the compressed ROM
        02  Compress the file from the uncompressed ROM
        03  Marks the file as deleted

    The length of the array is the chunk length. The length of the array must
    equal the DMA entry count or be rejected.

Ocarina of Time Randomizer Patch File Chunk [RAPF]

    This is an example chunk for how the format could be extended.

    This chunk stores a ZPF (Zelda Patch File, see reference below) that must
    be applied to the decompressed ROM before it is compressed.

